  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 135 PM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe long term will be mostly controlled by high pressure\nridging, though several embedded disturbances will help to\ndisrupt this feature from growing stronger and brining much\nwarmer conditions, while modest moisture should bring at least a\nweak chance of precipitation through this period. The long term\nstarts with upper level high pressure controlling much of the \nsoutheastern US and extending up towards our region, while a\nweak low off of the southwestern California coastline moves \ninland and up towards the Four Corners region while continuing \nto weaken. This system, while weak and mostly non-impactful, \nwill at least help to stave off the ridge from strengthening \nover the region while also bringing a quick and weak shot of \nmoisture that should fuel a few weak upper elevation showers in \nthe region. We will be clearing out on Monday again before flow \nturns more southerly to southwesterly on Tuesday as a deep \nPacific low begins to approach the west coast and then moves \ninland through the end of the forecast period. This will \ncontinue to stream moisture in, allowing for further increasing\nchances of upslope induced precipitation that will be aided by \nembedded weak shortwaves within the flow, though once again not\nexpecting significant accumulations from this activity. Under \nthis pattern look for temperatures to remain largely the same \nthrough the long term as highs sit generally 5-10 degrees above \nnormal (60's to upper 70's), with portions of eastern Wyoming \nand the Nebraska Panhandle nearing 80 degrees, but currently not\nexpected to reach this warm. Otherwise, with no significant \nfeatures present over our area, look for largely benign \nconditions until likely just beyond the long term into the \nweekend after next. There is overall moderate to high confidence\nin the forecast as ensembles and their clusters are largely \nsimilar in their expectations of the evolution of this pattern, \nthough discrepancies begin to arise on how quickly the Pacific \nlow at the end of the forecast will move inland, but this \nshouldn't create drastic changes at least to this forecast \nperiod.",
